# Documentation protocol

After any substantive work, update **all** relevant documentation: markdown files **and** in-code comments. This protocol ensures the project stays internally consistent and self-documenting.

## Core rule: docs AND comments travel together

When you change logic, APIs, types, data flow, or behavior in a source file, you must also update:

1. **In-code comments**: JSDoc on the function/type you changed, plus any inline comments that describe the old behavior. If a function's purpose, parameters, return value, or side effects have changed, the JSDoc must reflect the new state.
2. **Documentation files**: whichever of the files below are affected (see sections).

If you add a **new** exported function, type, component, hook, or API route, it must have a JSDoc block before it is committed.

## MANDATORY: Change log stays current (local file)

`docs/change-log.md` is **gitignored**. The **`post-commit`** hook (see `.githooks/README.md`) appends a **Part 2** row after each successful `git commit` with the real short hash and subject.

**Agent checklist after `git commit`:** Confirm a new Part 2 row exists for `HEAD`. If hooks were off or `--no-verify` was used, append the row manually (`git rev-parse --short=7 HEAD`, `git log -1 --format=%s`). Refine the hook’s summary or attribution when the default line is insufficient.

See the "How to maintain" section at the top of `docs/change-log.md` for table format. When in doubt, log substantive work; purely mechanical churn can stay as the hook’s minimal line unless the owner wants more detail.

**Drift repair:** If your local Part 2 is behind `git log`, backfill rows in chronological order (matching `git log`) before relying on the file as a journal.

## After every error fix

Add a new entry to `docs/error-log.md` using the template at the top of that file. Include:
- Date, category, severity
- Symptom (what the user saw)
- Root cause (why it happened)
- Fix applied (what changed)
- Prevention notes (how to avoid it in the future)

If the error is a recurring pitfall, also update the "Issues encountered and fixes" table in `docs/change-log.md`.

## After UI changes

If any user-visible UI has changed (layout, styling, new components, modified interactions, new states):
- Update the relevant section in `docs/ui-lookbook.md`, preserving the previous state (mark it as "Previous") and describing the new state
- Take new desktop (1440×900) and mobile (390×844) screenshots with full-page scrolling and save to `docs/screenshots/`
- Update any inline comments in the component that describe layout, states, or interaction behavior

## After architecture changes

If routes, integrations, data flow, environment variables, dependencies, or file structure have changed:
- Update the relevant section in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- If new files were added, update the file map section
- If new env vars were added, update the environment variables section and `.env.example`
- Update file-level JSDoc in any new or restructured files

## After code audit / refactor changes

If functions are moved, renamed, un-exported, or consolidated:
- Update `docs/code-audit.md` with the finding number, status, and verification result
- Update JSDoc and inline comments in the affected files to reflect the new structure
- Run `npm run build` to verify zero TypeScript errors before committing

## Documentation file reference

| File | What it tracks |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/change-log.md` | Git commit history (both repos), anti-regression table |
| `docs/error-log.md` | Error history with root causes and prevention notes |
| `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Tech stack, file map, data flow, env vars, system design |
| `docs/ui-lookbook.md` | Visual documentation of every UI state and interaction |
| `docs/code-audit.md` | Code redundancy/efficiency findings with implementation status |
| `docs/CODE_REVIEW.md` | Code review process, checklists, and session records |
| `docs/BUILD_PHASES.md` | Original build plan (historical reference) |
| `docs/BROWSER_VERIFICATION.md` | Browser testing procedures |
